The '''2023 UEFA Super Cup''' was the 48th edition of the [[UEFA Super Cup]], an annual [[association football|football]] match organised by [[UEFA]] and contested by the reigning champions of the top two European club competitions, the [[UEFA Champions League]] and the [[UEFA Europa League]]. The match featured English club [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]], winners of the [[2022–23 UEFA Champions League]], and Spanish club [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], winners of the [[2022–23 UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at the [[Karaiskakis Stadium]] in [[Piraeus]], Greece, on 16 August 2023.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://editorial.uefa.com/resources/0279-163596ae271e-e04faa20e08e-1000/20220926_circular_2022_65_en.zip |title=International match calendar and access list for the 2023/24 season |number=65/2022 |magazine=UEFA Circular Letter |publisher=[[UEFA|Union of European Football Associations]] |date=26 September 2022 |access-date=27 September 2022}}</ref>

The match was originally scheduled to be played at the [[Ak Bars Arena]] in [[Kazan]], Russia. However, due to the [[Russian invasion of Ukraine]], it was moved on 25 January 2023 to Athens.

Manchester City won the match 5–4 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]]  following a 1–1 draw after 90 minutes for their first UEFA Super Cup title.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0284-18c07e15b0ff-af3118a0e297-1000/ |title=Manchester City 1–1 Sevilla (5–4 pens): City claim first Super Cup in shoot-out drama |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=16 August 2023 |access-date=16 August 2023}}</ref>

